ing  all which can get to the right answer of  a given question so so no but of course  some ways of thinking are better than  others and if we didn't accept that we  wouldn't be in a position really to to  criticize any way of thinking I mean as  for the waking up from Western prejudice  I suspect that talking about Western  prejudice would be about as useful as  talking about Eastern prejudice it's  just lumping so many different things  together that it's not a very helpful  category I mean I think in our thinking  we make all kinds of assumptions that we  that we don't reflect on then then that  prejudice in that sense is something you  simply can't avoid because you could  never be fully reflective and aware of  everything that you're assuming and  aware of everything you're assuming in  the in in that awareness and so on ad  infinitum so you know I think it's also  it's also the case that talking about  waking up from prejudice makes it sound  much easier than it really is because it  makes it sound as though all you really  have to do is a prick yourself with a  pin and you wake up from your prejudices  but actually I identifying ways in which  you've been relying on false assumptions  which is that's the key thing it's a  very important thing to do but it's also  a very difficult thing to do thank you  yeah thanks I just want to say that I'm  interested in international politics so  for ideas for me matter in what they do  politically and therefore I find the  question quite interesting because I  don't think that's one right way to  think I agree with Tim and Rahm but if  there was one then this the formulation  of this question suggests that that way  is Western so do we need to wake up from  Western privilege or is there only one  right way to think it doesn't allow you  to say actually maybe the only right way  to think is an African traditional  thought or Eastern traditional for not  that I'd like to say that but it signals  that Western hegemony is so